pp|xyz)\\\\b\",\n    \"/workspace\\\\b\",\n    \"/users/\",\n    \"/home/\",\n    \"sk-or-\",\n    \"\\\\bsk-[a-z0-9_-]{16,}\",\n    \"key[^\\\\n]{0,24}(fingerprint|sha256|sha-256|hash)[^\\\\n]{0,12}[0-9a-f]{8,}\"\n  ],\n  \"deny_phrases\": [\n    \"Ratified proceed\",\n    \"I agree with all of these\",\n    \"Item 29 - (A) approved as recommended\",\n    \"Item 28 - (B) fix it in this job\",\n    \"Remaining - (A) approved as recommended\",\n    \"Adopt the tripwire: after the same Maker failure 3 times, stop and change the request\",\n    \"Keep the shared key with the spend guard\",\n    \"Make the cross-family pre-read optional, decided per job\",\n    \"Use the same openrouter key I gave you last time\",\n    \"Item 30: the Maker writes the field guide in this job, with bar checks for it\",\n    \"Paraphrase my words as Owner decisions; no verbatim quotes in the doc\",\n    \"When I said three iterations, I was talking about three iterations of the EDI loop\",\n    \"Looks like you choked the evaluator model\",\n    \"don't put your work in /tmp\",\n    \"As Owner I give disposition: accepted\",\n    \"stop choking the model\",\n    \"run the controls and instrument from a writable copy of freeze/ with byte-identical, hash-verified files\",\n    \"I accept iteration 2 with the gaps logged\",\n    \"ratified (and yes, PRIOR makes sense)\",\n    \"amend the bar as Buyer to extend the time bound so gen 3 can run\",\n    \"Amend P5(e) so headers carry the build time\",\n    \"As Buyer I amend the bar to raise the cap from 3 to 4 generations\",\n    \"As Buyer I amend the cap to 3 E-DI iterations, with Maker attempts limited only by budget\",\n    \"As Buyer I approve AMENDMENT-4\",\n    \"I approve the sheet as-is\",\n    \"Tighten N1 to use the logger's send and receive times\",\n    \"Close job 2026-10-27-custody-emitter-gaps and write its CL1 custody record\",\n    \"under $5 in LLM processing and under 2hrs of my time\"\n  ],\n  \"deny_phrase_rule\": \"a phrase matches after normalization and after dropping every character that is not a letter, digit or space (so punctuation and quote marks do not matter)\"\n}\n```\n\n## Input 1: the Owner's draft (owner-draft.md, sha256 fbf070f52a6ca6978604847d6afdcf5d469029ca5e143827291236940c31e344)\n\n```\n# Running a job as Orchestrator: a field guide\n\nStatus: **working document. Non-normative.** It describes practice that has worked, and the failures behind it. It is not part of the doctrine or the custody record, and nothing here overrides either. Where it disagrees with the doctrine, the doctrine wins.\n\nDraft of 2026-10-28. Original author: GrokBot, from the Plumbline job `2026-10-27-custody-emitter-gaps` (still open when this was written). Review and additions: VizH, from the evaluatordriven custody jobs v0.9.6 and v0.9.7. Passages added from the review are marked **[added]** or **[qualified]**.\n\nRead this before you open a job. Each rule has a story behind it, from a real job where the rule was broken. The stories are here so you can reason from the why when you meet a case the rules don't cover.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What you are, and what you are not\n\nYou are the **Orchestrator**. You write the bar with the Owner, freeze it, open the job, route work between seats, apply the pass rule mechanically, keep the record, and bring decisions to the Owner.\n\nYou are **not** the Maker and **not** the Evaluator. You never write the fix, never make the Maker's model calls, never score. If you catch yourself \"just fixing one line\" in the subject, stop: that removes the separation the method exists to prove.\n\n**[added]** If the same model that wrote an instrument also runs the gate it is judged by, say so in the record's separation gaps. Where the seats can be split, split them: in the evaluatordriven jobs the Architect wrote the bar and the instruments, and a different model ran, logged and relayed, and wrote only facts in its notes, never diagnoses.\n\nThe Owner holds Owner, Buyer and Specialist. Only the Owner's own words approve anything. Another agent saying \"the Owner said yes\" is a *report*, not an approval. Record it as relayed, with time and channel, and say you did not witness it.\n\n## 2. The words that matter\n\n- **Iteration** = one turn of the loop: the Maker produces, the Evaluator scores, you apply the pass rule. **The cap counts iterations.** The Owner, 2026-10-28, on the Plumbline job: the cap he meant was iterations of the loop.\n- **Maker-internal attempts** (retries, follow-up calls, regenerations inside one Maker turn) are not iterations. Let the Maker loop inside its money and time budget.\n  - *Story:* the bar said \"cap 3 generations.\" Generations 1 and 2 failed for plumbing reasons (a token cap, malformed diffs) before the Evaluator scored anything. When iteration 1 failed scoring, the bar said the attempts were gone, and the Owner had to amend the cap to allow a second real iteration. Write bars as \"cap N iterations; Maker attempts limited by budget.\"\n  - **[qualified]** Add a tripwire. In the v0.9.6 job a limit on attempts is what exposed a transport failure (9 of 10 attempts failed at \"the diff does not apply\"). Unlimited retries would have hidden it behind budget. When the same failure class repeats (for example three times), stop retrying and change the delivery contract. Log every attempt.\n- **Bar** = the frozen standard (criteria, instruments, pass rule, bounds). Its digest is what the Buyer approves.\n- **Frozen set** = everything listed in `custody/MANIFEST.sha256`. Changing any of it after freeze is a **Buyer amendment**, never a quiet edit.\n\n## 3. Before you freeze: the checklist\n\nMost of the pain on the first job came from things that should have been caught before freeze. Do every item.\n\n1. **Don't throttle any model.** No reasoning limit, and max output tokens = the endpoint's own maximum (look it up on the provider's endpoints page and save a snapshot). This applies to the Maker and to any model inside an Evaluator instrument.\n   - *Story 1:* a Maker generation had a 24k token cap. The model spent 19.9k thinking and returned nothing usable.\n   - *Story 2:* an instrument was frozen with `--max-tokens 800`. All three scoring calls spent about 798 tokens reasoning and returned nothing. The batch was void.\n   - **[qualified]** The maximum is necessary, not sufficient. In an evaluatordriven job one request for 12 records failed five times with `finish=length` at 131,072 tokens, almost all reasoning and no content, at about $0.59 per call. What fixed it was batching (four calls of three records), and treating `finish=length` with empty content as its own failure class: stop and change the request. Size each task so the model can finish inside the maximum, and log `finish_reason` on every call.\n2. **Run every model-calling instrument live, at the exact frozen settings, before freezing.** Dry runs and mocks do not catch a starved model. One real call per instrument, confirm a parseable verdict, attach the output, then freeze. **[added]** In the evaluatordriven job the deterministic instruments were dry-run (64 of 64 as expected) and the records splitter was not run live, which is how five paid calls failed after the freeze.\n3. **Write the Maker's output contract into the bar.** Say \"whole files between markers\" explicitly. Models write whole files reliably and write unified diffs badly.\n   - *Story:* generation 2 returned four complete-but-corrupt diffs, none of which applied. Generation 3 used whole files via `extract_files.py` and passed the accept rule on call 2.\n   - **[added]** The same failure happened independently in the evaluatordriven jobs: 9 of 10 diff attempts failed to apply in v0.9.6. After extraction, check the diff scope against the allow-list, so a rewritten file cannot carry unrelated edits.\n4. **State the time bound as a clock time**, for example \"ends 2026-10-28 13:15 EDT\", not \"under 10 hours\". Derive every cutoff from it in the handoffs and show the arithmetic.\n   - *Story:* a call-start cutoff and the job's end were mixed up, and nobody could tell which number was the real limit.\n5. **Plan the approval checkpoints against the clock.** Every Maker or Evaluator model run needs the Owner's yes. If he is asleep, the clock keeps running.\n   - *Story:* a generation waited overnight for a yes, and the job overran its time bound before the call started. Either schedule runs when the Owner is around, or get the yeses up front for a defined sequence.\n   - **[added]** Plan against any decision cap too. An evaluatordriven bar set a cap of 10 Owner decisions and the job used 12. A standing ruling for a defined sequence (for example \"proceed, uncapped, three per session\") saved several asks.\n6. **Enforce spend in the harness, not in prose.** Ceilings written in a handoff are procedural; nothing stops a runaway call. Put per-call and per-batch caps in the tool.\n   - **[added]** Use a per-job API key with its own limit, so the provider enforces the ceiling and the job's spend reconciles exactly with the key's usage. An evaluatordriven job shared a key with other work: the ledger and the key's total never reconciled, and the key's own limit stopped a call partway through.\n7. **[added] Get a pre-read from a different model family before work starts.** The reviewer reads the bar, the runbook and the instruments, and lists what is unclear or unfair. Fix or justify every finding. In the evaluatordriven jobs this took findings from 35 down to 0 over successive rounds. It catches wording and gate problems. It cannot catch a starved model, so it complements the live run in item 2 and does not replace it.\n\n## 4. Running the loop\n\n**Maker turn**\n- Hand off with exact preconditions: branch head sha, ledger sha, prompt file sha, harness sha, log entry count, clock cutoff. If any doesn't match, the Maker stops. That is a feature.\n- Give the Maker the Evaluator's findings from the previous iteration: instrument reasons and the model judge's reply. **Redact anything from held-out controls.** The Maker must never see them.\n- **[added]** The shortfall comes from the Evaluator, filtered by script, and you relay it unedited. Do not rewrite, summarize or diagnose it.\n- When the Maker reports, **verify it yourself** (or have your worker do it): re-extract the files from the raw model reply and confirm they are byte-identical to the commit, check paths against the allow-list, re-run the tests, reconcile spend with key usage. Trust, then verify.\n\n**Evaluator turn**\n- Give the Evaluator a **writable** copy of the controls and check integrity with the manifest before and after.\n  - *Story:* the copy was made read-only to \"protect\" it. The scoring harness copies fixtures with their permissions, so the subject's emitter could not write its output, and five checks failed for environmental reasons. Batch 1 was void.\n- The batch seal (BATCH-MANIFEST) must not list itself.\n- Read key usage at least 2 to 3 minutes after the last call, or the last call's cost will not show yet.\n- When results look strange, **ask \"is this the subject or my setup?\"** before you apply the pass rule. A void batch is recorded as void with its causes. It is not a score.\n- Even in a void batch, look for real signal. In batch 1, two checks failed on a fixture the setup bug did not touch. That was a true result and it held in batch 2.\n- **[added]** An instrument that settles a split between readers needs the same care as any other. Probe it on malformed and missing-field inputs before it settles anything, and make it implement the specification's rule for them. In an evaluatordriven job the tiebreak instrument did not, and it settled a split the wrong way.\n\n**Applying the pass rule**\n- Mechanical: every dimension must pass. Report what failed, quote the instrument's reason in plain words, and say what it means for the work.\n- At the cap, the state is `needs_owner`. Bring the Owner the options (amend, reject) with your recommendation and the clock arithmetic for each.\n\n## 5. Records\n\n- The job record lives **in the product repo** beside the code: `dot-plumbline/jobs/<job_id>/` (doctrine rule 12). Not in scratch.\n- **[added]** That includes the job log, the evidence it cites, and the instruments that accepted the work. Commit them redacted where a held-out control would leak, with the hash of the full copy beside it. In an evaluatordriven job the record was committed but the log, 130 evidence files and the acceptance instruments stayed local, so a public check of the clone read NOT-CHECKABLE on two requirements, and the job had called that expected.\n- The ledger (`inputs/custody-events.jsonl`) is append-only. Every amendment, generation open and close, and evaluation verdict gets a line; record the ledger sha after each append and pin it in the next handoff. The ledger tool cannot yet write generation, evaluation or amendment lines, so they were hand-appended (follow-up F1: add commands).\n- **[added]** Keep logged files write-once, by content. Before an event cites a file, copy it to `evidence/<name>-<sha8>.<ext>` and cite the copy. A correction is a new file and a new event, never an edit.\n- Post-freeze amendments go **beside** the frozen set (job root), never inside `custody/`, so the bar digest stays valid. Each amendment quotes the Owner's words verbatim, with time, channel, and whether you witnessed it.\n- **[added]** Every Owner decision gets that treatment, not only amendments: words, time, channel, witnessed or relayed. A cap on decisions is only as good as the log behind it. One evaluatordriven decision could not be attributed to any event.\n- Model-judge logs: commit them **redacted** (requests and replies, since replies can quote control code), with the full log's sha alongside. The full log stays outside the repo.\n- The API key is referenced by path only. Scan for it before every push.\n- Git identity: author \"E-DI Maker\" for Maker commits, committer the Owner, with his noreply address.\n\n## 6. Working with the Owner\n\n- He decides; you recommend. Lead with the answer, give your recommendation, and offer real options. Show the clock math when time matters.\n- When he gives a principle (\"don't choke the model\", \"the cap counts loop iterations\"), apply it everywhere it fits, not just where he said it, and write it down here.\n- Own your mistakes plainly. \"I froze an 800-token cap\" beats explaining.\n- Push only on his authority, and cite the message.\n- **[added]** If you are checking a live site, send `Accept: text/html`. A bare `curl` sends `*/*` and can miss a script injected only for HTML requests.\n\n## 7. Open items\n\n- [ ] Record the outcome of the remaining iterations on the job above.\n- [ ] Fold this guide into the job record when the job closes.\n- [ ] Update the E-DI skill and the Orchestrator runbook from sections 2 to 5.\n- [ ] Follow-up job: F1 (ledger commands), F7 (fixture modes), F8 (enforced spend caps).\n- [ ] Before this is published: scan it (no keys, no private names), and decide whether the Owner's quoted words stay as quotes or become paraphrase.\n```\n\n## Input 2: the Orchestrator's guide (orchestrator-guide.md, sha256 01fe4e161dc5e56dca9e5dac0c4de706798207b4a680d13d154e100e95577e13)\n\n```\n# Running an E-DI job as Orchestrator: a field guide\n*Written for whoever runs the next one. Started 2026-10-28 during Plumbline job `2026-10-27-custody-emitter-gaps`; closed `accepted` 2026-10-28 10:44 EDT and merged to main (PR #3, b8fef62) at 10:45 EDT. This copy is final for that job; its rules are proposed for the E-DI skill (`SKILL-proposed.md`). Updated 2026-10-28 ~16:00 EDT with VizH's review (supplied by Steve 15:49 EDT): rules from it are tagged \"(VizH, …)\" and proposed for the skill in `SKILL-proposed-vizh.md`; agreement is in section 9; the three points Steve decided at 15:52 EDT are rules in section 10.*\n\nRead this before you open a job. Every rule below has a story behind it, and the story is from a real job where the rule was broken. The stories are here so you understand *why*, because you will meet cases these rules don't cover and you'll need to reason from the why.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What you are, and what you are not\n\nYou are the **Orchestrator**. You write the bar with the Owner, freeze it, open the job, route work between seats, apply the pass rule mechanically, keep the record, and bring decisions to the Owner. That's it.\n\nYou are **not** the Maker and **not** the Evaluator. You never write the fix, never make the Maker's model calls, never score. If you catch yourself \"just fixing one line\" in the subject, stop: that destroys the separation the whole method exists to prove.\n\nSteve holds Owner, Buyer and Specialist (he signs as **S2**). Only his own words approve anything. Another bot telling you \"Steve said yes\" is a *report*, not an approval. Record it as relayed, with time and channel, and say you didn't witness it.\n- **If the same seat writes an instrument and also runs the gate it's judged by, or applies the pass rule to its output, say so in the record's `separationGaps`.** *(VizH, qualification 4.)* Why: separation is the thing the record exists to prove. If one seat built the ruler and also reads it, a reader has to know that to weigh the result. Our seating isn't wrong, but it isn't the strongest one, so disclose it instead of letting a reader assume it.\n  - *Story from VizH's v0.9.6/v0.9.7 jobs:* an Architect (VizH) wrote the bar and the instruments. A separate Orchestrator, a different model, only ran, logged and relayed, and wrote facts only, never a diagnosis. The rule there was that whoever writes an instrument must not run the steps it judges.\n  - *Ours:* in both Plumbline jobs you, the Orchestrator, wrote the bar and the instruments (`run_instrument.sh`, `d6_batch.py`, `rubric-D6.md`, the guarded `or_call.py`), the Evaluator bot ran them, and you applied the pass rule. The doctrine-pointer record's `separationGaps` discloses the Maker operator, the shared-box controls and the hand-typed lines, but **not** that the instruments and the pass rule sit in one seat. From the next job on, add that line.\n\n## 2. The words that matter\n\n- **Iteration** = one turn of the E-DI loop: the Maker produces, the Evaluator scores, you apply the pass rule. **The cap counts iterations.** Steve, 2026-10-28: \"When I said three iterations, I was talking about three iterations of the EDI loop.\"\n- **Maker-internal attempts** (retries, follow-up calls, regenerations inside one Maker turn) are *not* iterations. Let the Maker loop as much as it needs **inside its money and time budget**, up to the repeat-failure tripwire (the same failure class 3 times means stop and change the request; section 10a). Don't count them against the cap.\n  - *Story:* on this job I wrote the bar as \"cap 3 generations.\" Generations 1 and 2 failed for plumbing reasons (token cap, malformed diffs) before the Evaluator ever scored anything. When iteration 1 finally failed scoring, the bar said we were out of attempts. Steve had to amend the cap (AMENDMENT-5) just to get a second real iteration. Write the cap as in the next bullet.\n- **Write the cap in the bar in exactly these words: \"cap N E-DI iterations; Maker attempts limited by budget, the clock-time bound, and the repeat-failure tripwire (same failure class 3x -> stop and change the request).\"** *(VizH, qualification 3.)* Why: a bare count (\"3 generations\", \"3 revisions\") gets read as counting Maker sessions or round-trips. Then plumbing failures use up the cap before the Evaluator has scored anything. Naming both counts in one sentence leaves no room to misread it.\n  - *Story (ours):* AMENDMENT-5, above.\n  - *Story from VizH's v0.9.6/v0.9.7 jobs:* their bars said \"3 revisions,\" which counted Maker sessions and round-trips, and the loop ran out of attempts for plumbing reasons before the Evaluator had scored anything. Two teams hit the same failure independently.\n  - The tripwire part is Steve's decision of 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT (section 10a). It replaces \"Maker attempts limited only by budget\": attempts are still not iterations, but the third occurrence of one failure class stops the retries.\n- **Bar** = the frozen standard (criteria, instruments, pass rule, bounds). Its digest is what the Buyer approves.\n- **Frozen set** = everything listed in `custody/MANIFEST.sha256`. Changing any of it after freeze is a **Buyer amendment**, never a quiet edit.\n\n## 3. Before you freeze: the checklist\n\nMost of this job's pain came from things that should have been caught before freeze. Do every item.\n\n1. **Don't throttle any model.** No reasoning limit, and max output tokens = the endpoint's own maximum (look it up on the provider's endpoints page and save a snapshot). This applies to the Maker **and** to any model inside an Evaluator instrument.\n   - *Story 1:* Maker gen 1 had a 24k token cap; the model spent 19.9k thinking and returned nothing usable.\n   - *Story 2:* I froze `--max-tokens 800` inside the GLM scoring instrument. All three calls spent ~798 tokens reasoning and returned empty. The batch was void and Steve had to approve AMENDMENT-4. His words: \"Looks like you choked the evaluator model.\"\n2. **Run every model-calling instrument live, at the exact frozen settings, before freezing.** Dry runs and mocks don't catch a starved model. One real call per instrument, confirm you get a parseable verdict, then freeze.\n3. **Write the Maker's output contract into the bar.** Say \"whole files between markers\" explicitly. Models write whole files reliably and write unified diffs badly.\n   - *Story:* gen 2 returned four complete-but-corrupt diffs; none applied. Gen 3 used whole files via `extract_files.py` and passed the operator's accept rule on call 2. The bar said \"patch,\" which left me interpreting (D6). Don't leave the Owner's intent to interpretation.\n4. **State the time bound as a clock time**, e.g. \"ends 2026-10-28 13:15 EDT,\" not \"under 10 hours.\" Then derive every cutoff from it in the handoffs and show the arithmetic.\n   - *Story:* I mixed up a call-start cutoff (02:15) with the job's end (which I said was 03:15, but strictly from open was 05:44). Nobody could tell which number was the real limit.\n5. **Plan the approval checkpoints against the clock.** Every Maker or Evaluator model run needs Steve's yes in that bot's chat. If he's asleep, the clock keeps running.\n   - *Story:* gen 3 waited overnight for a yes, and the job blew its time bound before the call started (AMENDMENT-3). Either schedule runs when Steve is around, or get the yeses up front for a defined sequence.\n6. **Enforce spend in the harness, not just in prose.** Ceilings written in a handoff are procedural; nothing stops a runaway call. Put per-call and per-batch caps in the tool (follow-up F8).\n7. **Size each task so the model can finish inside its maximum, and log `finish_reason` on every call.** *(VizH, qualification 1.)* Item 1 (max tokens = the endpoint's maximum) is **necessary, not sufficient**. It removes the cap *you* set, not the endpoint's own ceiling. A task too big for one reply burns the whole allowance on reasoning and returns no content, at full price. So split the work into calls that fit with room to spare (batches, or one group of files per call), and have the harness log `finish_reason` along with the reasoning and content token counts for every call. That way a starved call shows up in the log and nobody has to guess.\n   - *Story from VizH's v0.9.6/v0.9.7 jobs:* one records call asked for 12 records in a single reply. It failed five times with `finish=length` at 131,072 tokens, almost all reasoning and zero content, at about $0.59 a call. Batching fixed it: four calls of three records each.\n   - *Ours:* the guarded harness already logs `finish` (doctrine-pointer iter 1: `stop`, 21,753 / 26,185 tokens). Keep it that way, and make the sizing part of the bar, not luck.\n8. **Make the live run a gate on the bar's checklist, with its output attached.** *(VizH, add 3.1.)* This hardens item 2. Make one real call per model-calling instrument at the exact frozen settings: model, provider pin, max tokens, prompt template. Save the request, reply, `finish_reason` and cost to a file, and cite that file's sha on the checklist. No attached output, no freeze. Why: a rule that lives only in prose gets skipped under time pressure, and an attached file can be checked by someone who wasn't there.\n   - *Story from VizH's v0.9.6/v0.9.7 jobs:* they dry-ran the deterministic instruments (64 of 64 as expected) but never ran the records splitter live. Five paid calls failed after the freeze.\n   - *Ours:* the 800-token GLM freeze (item 1, story 2) is the same miss. The doctrine-pointer job did it right: both pinned providers were live-tested before freeze, and the output is committed under `custody/livetest/`.\n9. **Show every referee or checker a malformed input before you trust it on a good one.** *(VizH, add 3.6.)* Any instrument that settles a question (a checker, a tiebreak, a schema validator, a verifier) must first be fed records with missing fields, malformed fields and wrong types. It has to fail them the way the spec says. Why: a checker that has only seen good records may pass anything. Planted controls test whether a judge can tell good work from bad. This tests whether the instrument handles broken input at all.\n   - *Story from VizH's v0.9.6/v0.9.7 jobs:* two adjudicators split on a question. The tiebreak instrument didn't implement the spec's rule for missing and malformed fields, and it settled the split the wrong way.\n   - *Ours:* item 29 of the custody-times job (each `plumbline_verify.py` failure type gets its own planted case) applies this rule. The doctrine-pointer D6 note, one kind of planted mistake only, is a cousin of it.\n10. **Log every Owner decision as it happens, and plan approvals against any decision cap as well as the clock.** *(VizH, add 3.4.)* Each line gets the time with zone, the channel or message id, the words verbatim, and whether you **witnessed** it or it was **relayed**. If the bar caps Owner decisions, count your planned checkpoints against that cap when you draft the sheet. A standing ruling for a defined sequence of runs saves asks. Why: a cap is only as good as the log behind it, and a decision you reconstruct later can't be attributed.\n   - *Story from VizH's v0.9.6/v0.9.7 jobs:* the bar capped Owner decisions at 10 and the job used 12. The log had ten by event, two came by chat, and one of the ten couldn't be tied to any event. A standing ruling (\"proceed, uncapped, three per session\") saved them several asks.\n   - *Ours:* `OWNER-APPROVAL-RECORD.md` (doctrine-pointer) is this log, e.g. 11:53 EDT t81s455 and 11:58 EDT t82u, verbatim. Item 16's up-front approval of iterations 1–3 was our standing ruling. The seats' go-aheads (12:36, 12:53 EDT) are logged as relays, not witnessed.\n11. **When a job touches a live site, check pages with a browser-style `Accept` header** (`curl -H 'Accept: text/html' …`). *(VizH, add 3.8.)* Why: servers and CDNs vary the response by `Accept`, and a bare `curl` sends `*/*`, so you can be checking a page no browser ever gets.\n    - *Story from VizH's v0.9.6/v0.9.7 jobs:* a bare `curl` missed a script that Cloudflare injected only into HTML requests. We have no story of our own yet.\n\n**Freeze gate: tick every box before you freeze** *(adapted from VizH's checklist, with Steve's 15:52 EDT decisions in section 10)*\n- [ ] Every model-calling instrument run live at the frozen settings, with the output attached and its sha cited (item 8).\n- [ ] Each task sized to finish inside the model's maximum; `finish_reason` logged on every call (item 7).\n- [ ] No throttle: max tokens = the endpoint maximum from a saved snapshot, and no reasoning limit (item 1).\n- [ ] Maker delivers whole files between markers; the runbook names the command that *writes* them; the diff scope is checked against the allow-list after extraction.\n- [ ] Cap written as \"cap N E-DI iterations; Maker attempts limited by budget, the clock-time bound, and the repeat-failure tripwire (same failure class 3x -> stop and change the request)\"; failure classes logged per attempt (sections 2, 10a).\n- [ ] Spend enforced in the harness: per-call and per-batch caps plus the pre-call guard on the shared key, baseline set and refusal tested before freeze; the record will call key-usage reconciliation an upper bound (item 6, section 10b).\n- [ ] Cross-family pre-read decided on the approval sheet; if yes, every finding fixed or justified in a committed file (section 10c).\n- [ ] Every referee/checker shown a malformed input and seen to fail it (item 9).\n- [ ] Owner decisions logged with time, channel, verbatim words, and witnessed or relayed; approvals planned against the clock and any decision cap (item 10, item 5).\n- [ ] Instruments and redacted logs (with the full log's sha) planned to be committed beside the work, not just the record (section 5).\n- [ ] Logged files are write-once by content: `evidence/<name>-<sha8>.<ext>` (section 5).\n- [ ] If one seat writes instruments and runs or applies the gate, a `separationGaps` line is drafted (section 1).\n\n## 4. Running the loop\n\n**Maker turn**\n- Hand off with exact preconditions: branch head sha, ledger sha, prompt file sha, harness sha, log entry count, clock cutoff. If any doesn't match, the Maker stops. That's a feature.\n- Give the Maker the Evaluator's findings from the previous iteration: instrument reasons and the model judge's reply. **Redact anything from held-out controls.** The Maker must never see them.\n- When the Maker reports, **verify it yourself** (or have your worker do it): re-extract the files from the raw model reply and confirm they're byte-identical to the commit, check paths against the allow-list, re-run the tests, reconcile spend with key usage. Trust, then verify.\n- **Treat `finish=length` with empty content as its own failure class: stop, resize the request, and don't retry it as is.** *(VizH, qualification 1.)* This is the one class where you don't wait for the tripwire's third time (section 10a), because an identical retry can't turn out differently. Why: the same request at the same maximum gives the same result and the same bill. It's a problem with the shape of the request, not bad luck, and not a failing subject. Do: the runbook names this case. The Maker stops retrying that request and changes it (a smaller task, or a split into several calls). The changed request is a new Maker attempt, not an iteration. If the change alters the delivery contract frozen in the bar, it's an amendment for Steve.\n  - *Story from VizH's v0.9.6/v0.9.7 jobs:* the 12-record call (section 3, item 7) was retried five times at 131,072 tokens. That's about $3 (5 × ~$0.59) before anyone changed the request.\n  - *Ours:* Maker gen 1 (24k cap) and the 800-token GLM batch both came back as reasoning with no content. Those were our caps, but the signature is the same. Read it as \"resize,\" not \"retry.\"\n\n- **When a Maker attempt breaks the tests, run the same tests at the unchanged base commit, in the same environment, before you blame anyone.** If the base passes, the break is in the Maker's files. If the base fails too, it's your setup.\n  - *Story:* in gen 5, call 2's files crashed the suite with a `FileNotFoundError`. It was the first run under the new `TMPDIR`, so the environment was a real suspect. The Maker ran one extra test at the unchanged base `b934b48` in a throwaway worktree, with the same `TMPDIR` and venv: 112 of 112 passed. That one run settled it. The crash was the Maker's, the failure text went back to the model, and call 4 was accepted. Record such a base run as a deviation, outside the repo, so it's on file but can't be mistaken for Maker evidence.\n- **\"The tests pass\" tells you the files fit the harness. It doesn't tell you *how* the finding was fixed. Read the diff and say what kind of fix it is.** Don't judge it (that's the Evaluator's job), but write it down plainly in the generation close line so the Owner isn't surprised.\n  - *Story:* iteration 2 failed N3 because a repository fixture's custody times were hand-typed, with no ledger line behind them. In gen 5 the Maker added four ledger events to that fixture, carrying the same hand-typed times, and relaxed the emitter's cross-checks so the new events would agree. The emitter still doesn't refuse or flag hand-typed times. The allow-list included `tests/fixtures/custody/`, so this was a legal move, and the easiest one. Whether that meets the bar is the Evaluator's call, but the question \"is a fixture-only fix acceptable?\" should have been settled in the prompt.\n  - Do instead: the allow-list is part of the spec. If the fix has to be in the code, leave fixtures off the allow-list, or say in the prompt that changing a fixture to make a finding go away doesn't count. Decide this with the Owner before the Maker turn, not after.\n- **Ask the Maker to quote the Owner's go-ahead word for word in its report.** You need that quote for the close line and the job record, and you didn't see it, because it happened in the Maker's own chat.\n  - *Story:* gen 5's report covered calls, costs and deviations in detail, but not Steve's yes. The close line could only say the go-ahead belongs in the Maker's chat and that the Orchestrator didn't witness it.\n- **A template copied forward carries its old words. Diff each filled handoff against the previous batch's handoff before you dispatch it.**\n  - *Story:* the batch-4 template still said \"The batch-3 dir `$B` does not exist yet\" in precondition E6. It was harmless, but a newcomer Evaluator could have checked the wrong folder. The filled copy fixes it.\n\n**Evaluator turn**\n- Give the Evaluator a **writable** copy of the controls and check integrity with the manifest before and after.\n  - *Story:* I made the copy read-only to \"protect\" it. The scoring harness copies fixtures with their file permissions, so the subject's emitter couldn't write its output, and five checks failed for environmental reasons. Batch 1 was void.\n- The batch seal (BATCH-MANIFEST) must not list itself.\n- Read key usage at least 2–3 minutes after the last call, or the last call's cost won't show yet.\n- When results look weird, **ask \"is this the subject or my setup?\"** before you apply the pass rule. A void batch is recorded as void with its causes; it is not a score.\n- Even in a void batch, look for real signal. In batch 1, N1 and N3 failed on a fixture the setup bug didn't touch. That was a true result and it held in batch 2.\n- **The control order rotates per batch, so read which call is the subject from the batch's own `order`. Don't assume a position.**\n  - *Story:* in batch 4, `n6/stdout.json` ran in the order [subject, G-c1, G-p1], so the subject was `n6-b4-1`, not `-2` as in an earlier batch. The verdict, the evidence copy and the close inputs all take the subject's call name from `items[].id == \"subject\"`.\n- **To check that the subject prompt was sent verbatim, rebuild it exactly as the harness does, numbered listing included.** A plain file dump will not match byte for byte, even when nothing is wrong.\n- **Read key usage well after the last call.** Batch 4 waited 156 s after the last call, and the after-read reconciled to the cent (the fix for F5's under-count).\n\n**When the box itself fails mid-run**\n- Your computer can reboot under you. On this job it rebooted at ~08:36 EDT during Maker gen 4, call 3: the call died with no exit file and no log entry, `/tmp` (including the test venv) was wiped, the scratch worktree vanished, and 44 committed files in an unrelated job folder showed as deleted.\n- What to do: (1) don't panic or re-run anything yet; (2) check what's actually damaged against git (`git status`, compare HEAD to origin, re-verify ledger and harness shas); (3) restore committed files from git, never from memory; (4) record the lost call as *lost*, with what you know (start time, likely billed cost from key usage) and never invent a log entry for it; (5) resume from the last good state.\n- **Never put job work in `/tmp`.** `/tmp` is wiped on reboot. Steve, 2026-10-28: \"don't put your work in /tmp.\"\n  - *Story:* our handoffs built the test venv at `/tmp/jsv`, the Maker ran its calls from wrapper scripts in `/tmp`, and follow-up rebuild checks wrote to `/tmp/check*.json`. The reboot took all of it, and the Maker had to rebuild before it could resume.\n  - Do instead: put venvs, wrapper scripts, scratch worktrees and any intermediate files under a job folder in `/workspace` (e.g. `/workspace/edi-work/<job_id>/`), which survives reboots. Write that path into every handoff's \"Names\" section.\n  - Commit evidence to the job branch as soon as each step finishes, so a crash can't take it. Uncommitted gen-4 evidence was one reboot away from being lost.\n- **Moving your paths out of `/tmp` is not enough; move the tools' temp files too. Set `TMPDIR`.** Python's `tempfile`, `mktemp` and most test suites write to `/tmp` on their own, whatever paths your handoff names.\n  - *Story:* after the reboot, the gen-5 handoff moved every named path to `/workspace/edi-work/…`, but nobody told the tests where to put *their* temp files. The Maker caught it during its pre-checks: it created `$W/tmp-maker-gen5`, exported `TMPDIR` to it, and only then ran anything. One test script alone left 32 temp folders there. Without that, they'd all have landed in `/tmp` again.\n  - Do instead: every handoff's \"Names\" section exports `TMPDIR` to a folder under the job's work root, one per seat and per run (`tmp-maker-gen5`, `tmp-eval-b4`). At close, list and clean those folders, and the old `/tmp` leftovers too (gen 4's `/tmp/jsv`, wrapper scripts and `check4.json` were still sitting there during gen 5).\n- **Every environment dependency outside the repo goes into the handoff preconditions, with a script to restore it.** If the tests need it and git doesn't hold it, a reboot can take it.\n  - *Story:* the test suite reads the E-DI corpus through `tools/pin.json`, at the Mac path `/Users/jarbis/Jarbis-PARAD/5_Devspace/edi`. On this box that path is a root-owned symlink to `/workspace/plumbline-job/src/e-di-a48a464`. No handoff mentioned it. The reboot wiped it, and the tests could not run until the Maker found the cause and recreated it with `sudo ln -s`, after checking the corpus files against the hashes in `tools/pin.json`.\n  - Do instead: at job open, list everything the tests and harness touch outside the repo (symlinks, corpora, venvs, CLIs, credentials *by path only*). Write a precondition for each that checks it (e.g. `readlink -f` gives the expected target and the `tools/pin.json` file hashes match). Keep a committed restore script that rebuilds it and then verifies it.\n- **Record exact tool and venv versions at job open, so a rebuild can match them.**\n  - *Story:* the test venv was rebuilt after the reboot (Python 3.13.5, jsonschema 4.26.0, attrs 26.1.0, jsonschema-specifications 2025.9.1, referencing 0.37.0, rpds-py 2026.9.1). Nothing written at job open said what the original held, so \"same as before\" could only be assumed, not checked. The identical test output was the only evidence that the rebuild matched.\n  - Do instead: at job open, commit an environment record to the job folder: `python --version`, `uv pip freeze` (or `pip freeze`) for each venv, `uv --version`, `git --version`, and the shas of any external corpus. Rebuild from that record with pinned versions (`uv pip install pkg==x.y.z …`), then diff the new freeze against it. Put that diff in the handoff as a precondition.\n\n**Applying the pass rule**\n- Mechanical: every dimension must pass. Report what failed, quote the instrument's reason in plain words, and say what it means for the work.\n- At the cap, the state is `needs_owner`. Bring Steve the options (amend, reject) with your recommendation and the clock arithmetic for each.\n\n## 5. Records\n\n- The job record lives **in the product repo** beside the code: `dot-plumbline/jobs/<job_id>/` (Doctrine rule 12). Not in scratch.\n- The ledger (`inputs/custody-events.jsonl`) is append-only. Every amendment, generation open/close and evaluation verdict gets a line; record the ledger sha after each append and pin it in the next handoff. The ledger tool can't yet write generation, evaluation or amendment lines, so we hand-appended them (F1: add commands).\n- Post-freeze amendments go **beside** the frozen set (job root), never inside `custody/`, so the bar digest stays valid and P5 still passes. Each amendment quotes Steve's words verbatim, with time, channel and whether you witnessed it.\n- Model-judge logs: commit them **redacted** (requests *and* replies, since replies can quote control code), with the full log's sha alongside. The full log stays outside the repo.\n- The OpenRouter key is referenced by path only. Scan for it before every push.\n- **Commit the instruments and the redacted logs beside the work, not just the record.** *(VizH, add 3.5.)* Doctrine §4 rule 12 says the record **and the instrument that accepted the work** are \"committed beside it, not in local scratch.\" Check that the job folder holds the scoring scripts, rubrics, harness, the redacted request and reply logs, and the full log's sha. Why: a stranger can only check what the clone contains, so an instrument kept in scratch makes those checks not checkable.\n  - *Story from VizH's v0.9.7 job:* the repo carried the record but not the job log, the evidence files or the acceptance instruments. A public check of the clone read NOT-CHECKABLE on CL1-8 and CL1-15. They had been calling that expected; VizH now calls it partly their own gap. Our redacted-logs-plus-full-sha practice is the fix VizH pointed to.\n  - *Ours:* the doctrine-pointer job folder has `custody/freeze/instrument/` (`run_instrument.sh`, `d6_batch.py`, `rubric-D6.md`), `custody/freeze/harness/or_call.py`, and the redacted E-GLM log with its full-log sha. Keep checking that, every job.\n- **Keep logged files write-once, by content.** *(VizH, add 3.7.)* Before a log event cites a file, copy it to `evidence/<name>-<sha8>.<ext>` (the first 8 hex of its sha256) and cite the copy. A correction is a new file and a new event, never an overwrite. At close, run the log's file check and say which cited files no longer match and why. Why: a file cited by path can change after the event, and then the log is wrong about what it saw.\n  - *Story from VizH's v0.9.6/v0.9.7 jobs:* this is VizH's working rule. The review gives it without a specific incident.\n  - *Ours:* while drafting the custody-times job we found that the emitter rewrites evidence logs in place, filling send and receive times from the server's clock, and that running the tests changes two committed fixture logs (sheet item 28). Content-named, write-once evidence would have made that change visible as a mismatch, not a silent edit.\n- Git identity: author \"E-DI Maker\" for Maker commits, committer Steve Moitozo II with his noreply address.\n\n**Preparing the close (a script, not a run)**\n- Close is `plumbline_job.py close` from **base main @ ff350e3, a clean checkout** with `PLUMBLINE_EXPECT_REVISION` set (bar: Emitter), not from the branch under test. It needs a corpus at the pin (`/workspace/plumbline-job/src/e-di-a48a464`) and a §5 **run file** per iteration. It writes the §5 record, the packet facts, a run-log row and then the custody record. The run file must live outside `inputs/`, because close copies it in (it would be copied onto itself).\n- `custody_ledger.py dispose` stamps **the time the tool runs**, not when Steve decided. To record his decision time, write it into `inputs/custody.json` and a matching `dispose` line by hand. The emitter refuses if the two disagree. Disclose the hand line.\n- The ff350e3 emitter has **no pass-through for extra `x-plumbline` fields**, so the bar's spend line and the emitter-limitation note can't go inside the record. Put them in a sidecar (`custody/record.x-plumbline-addendum.json`, which carries the record's sha) and in `separationGaps`. Tell Steve before the close, not after.\n- Custody CL1-16: **a script evaluator's script must not be the instrument's runner.** The first dry run failed CL1-16 when `run_instrument.sh` was both S1 and `instrument.runner`. Do instead: the runner is the written procedure that ran the evaluators (here the batch-4 Evaluator handoff), as in the custody-v1-emission precedent.\n- Held-out control case files are digested from their **absolute path** on the box. The record carries their sha only; the content never enters the repo. Disclose that a stranger can't re-read them.\n- Dry-run the whole close, commit included, on a scratch branch worktree under `/workspace/edi-work`. Test both `given` and `declined`, and check that the pre-checks refuse bad input. Then remove the worktree and delete the branch. The dry run is what found CL1-16.\n\n**After the close and the merge**\n- **Take every time floor from the log itself, never from a summary of it.** The close script refuses a decision time earlier than the last check, so that floor has to be the last check's *real* finish.\n  - *Story:* I first set the floor to 14:23:43Z from my batch notes. The dry-run record showed G-p1 at 14:24:26Z, which is later than my \"last\" time. The harness log said n6-b4-3 was received at 14:24:43Z and its generation reply fetched at 14:24:48Z. I moved the floor to 14:24:49Z, the last time in the log, rounded up. With the wrong floor, a decision time in that one minute would have dated Steve's yes before the check it was approving.\n- **Have the close ready before the Owner decides, so his yes turns into a record in seconds.** Steve gave disposition at 10:44:00 EDT (card t78s440). `CLOSE-apply.sh` committed the close (067c524) 28 seconds later: record `749b825b…`, check.py OK at CL1, P5(e) PASS. The PR was merged 57 seconds after that. Nothing was improvised at the moment that counted, because every surprise had already happened in the dry run.\n- **After the merge, the job branch is finished. Later changes to the job record go on a new branch cut from the merge commit.**\n  - *Story:* this guide's final sync came after PR #3 was merged. It went on `job-record/2026-10-27-custody-emitter-gaps-guide`, cut from main b8fef62, and touched only `ORCHESTRATOR-FIELD-GUIDE.md`. Adding commits to the merged job branch would have left them stranded off main.\n- **A merge changes the next job's starting point. Re-read the pins before drafting.** After b8fef62, main's emitter is the *fixed* one (gap 1 closed). So the next job's own record can be written by main at the merge commit, and the \"ff350e3 + producedAt limitation\" disclosure is no longer needed. Check, don't copy, the previous sheet's pins.\n\n## 6. Working with Steve\n\n- He decides; you recommend. Lead with the answer, give your recommendation, and offer real options on a card. Show the clock math when time matters.\n- When he gives a principle (\"stop choking the model,\" \"cap counts loop iterations\"), apply it everywhere it fits, not just where he said it, and write it down here.\n- Own your mistakes plainly. \"You're right, I froze an 800-token cap\" beats explaining.\n- Push only on his authority, and cite the message.\n\n## 7. This job's close: done\n- [x] Iteration 1 (gen 3, batch 2; batch 1 void): FAIL on N1, N3 and N6 (EVAL-ITER1-VERDICT.md). Iteration 2 (gen 4, batch 3): FAIL on N3 only (EVAL-ITER2-VERDICT.md). Iteration 3 (gen 5, batch 4): PASS on N1–N8, terminal state `accepted` (EVAL-ITER3-VERDICT.md).\n- [x] Owner disposition: given, 2026-10-28 10:44 EDT, card t78s440, verbatim: \"As Owner I give disposition: accepted. Close job 2026-10-27-custody-emitter-gaps and write its CL1 custody record.\" Closed by `CLOSE-apply.sh` in commit 067c524. Ledger `708359c6…9bcb`. Merged to main by PR #3 as b8fef62 (10:45 EDT), at Steve's request.\n- [x] Spend: US$0.303493375 in model calls, from key usage, reconciled per batch. Grok Bot's own spend can't be verified. Time: inside the 13:15 EDT bound, with about 2.5 h to spare.\n- [x] This guide is in the job record (`ORCHESTRATOR-FIELD-GUIDE.md`). Final sync on branch `job-record/2026-10-27-custody-emitter-gaps-guide`, from b8fef62.\n- [x] `/tmp` paths moved to `/workspace/edi-work/<job_id>/` in the iteration-3 handoffs, with `TMPDIR` exported there. The runbook version is in `SKILL-proposed.md`.\n- [x] Cleanup 2026-10-28 ~10:49 EDT. Removed gen 4's `/tmp` leftovers: `/tmp/jsv` (3.0 MB venv), `/tmp/edi-gen4-{budget,call,write}.sh`, `/tmp/base-test.txt` and `/tmp/check4.json`. Removed the `$W/tmp-*` folders `tmp-amend6`, `tmp-eval-b3`, `tmp-eval-b4`, `tmp-maker-gen5` (39 MB, mostly test temp dirs), `tmp-orch-b4` and `tmp-orch-gen5`. Kept on purpose: the corpus symlink, the venv at `$W/jsv`, the batch dirs, the key file and the empty `$W/tmp`. Gen 5's dangling scratch commits are left to `git gc`; none is on a branch.\n- [x] The E-DI skill update is drafted as `/workspace/edi-lessons/SKILL-proposed.md`, from sections 2–5. It is not applied; the Owner decides.\n\n## 8. Carried to next jobs\n- **Fixture-only fixes.** Is changing a fixture an acceptable fix for a finding about that fixture? Settle it in the bar and the allow-list before the Maker's first turn (section 4). For the doctrine-pointer job, the proposed answer is no: the allow-list is docs only.\n- **The x-plumbline sidecar.** ff350e3 couldn't write spend or limitation notes into the record, so this job used `custody/record.x-plumbline-addendum.json`. Is a sidecar enough for P6 spend? Main @ b8fef62 still has no pass-through, so either adopt the sidecar as the rule or add a pass-through (with the emitter follow-up job).\n- **Only the final iteration got a §5 record.** Iterations 1–2 live in EVAL-ITER1/2 and the ledger. Decide whether every iteration gets one.\n- **Follow-up job (queued second):** make the emitter refuse or flag hand-entered times (the real N3), plus F1 (ledger commands for generation, evaluation, amendment and dispose-at-decision-time), F7 (fixture modes) and F8 (spend caps enforced in the harness).\n\n## 9. Corroborated by VizH\n*VizH (Steve's Chief of Staff; Architect on the evaluatordriven v0.9.6 and v0.9.7 custody jobs) reviewed this guide. Steve supplied the review 2026-10-28 15:49 EDT. VizH found these independently, in different seating, so the agreement is evidence they're real. The stories are VizH's, not ours.*\n- **Whole files between markers, not diffs.** In v0.9.6, 9 of 10 Maker attempts failed at \"diff does not apply\" because hunk-header counts were off by 1 to 3, and none failed at a semantic gate. In v0.9.7, a round-trip that re-sent a full diff failed too. VizH's team dropped its plan to patch the diff parser and switched to whole files.\n- **Don't starve any model, including models inside instruments.** Their records call failed the same way our 800-token GLM scorer did (sections 3, item 7, and 4).\n- **Verify the Maker's report yourself.** They run acceptance on a copy of the tree and count a step only if it actually ran.\n- **A void batch is recorded as void with causes, not scored.** Same rule there, and they ask \"subject or setup?\" first.\n- **A bot's \"Steve said yes\" is a report, not an approval.** In their jobs the Owner runs the logging scripts himself, with his words verbatim.\n- **Put the output contract and the time bound in the bar, and don't leave the Owner's intent to interpretation.** Every ambiguity left in their bars came back as a ruling they had to ask for.\n- **Apply a principle everywhere it fits**, not only where Steve said it.\n\n## 10. Three rules Steve decided from VizH's review\n*VizH recommended these. They touched standing choices, so Steve decided them himself: 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT, card t95s532, witnessed in the Orchestrator's chat. His words are quoted with each rule.*\n\n**a) The repeat-failure tripwire: after the same Maker failure class 3 times, stop and change the request.**\n- Steve: \"Adopt the tripwire: after the same Maker failure 3 times, stop and change the request.\"\n- What to do: Maker attempts are still not iterations, and they are still limited by the budget and the clock-time bound. Now they are also limited by the tripwire. Log every attempt with its failure class: `finish=length`/empty, diff or extract won't apply, the same test failing, refused by provider, and so on. When the **same class** comes up a third time, the Maker stops retrying that request and changes it (a smaller task, a split, a different delivery contract) or reports back. If the change alters anything in the frozen set, it's an amendment for Steve. A different failure class starts its own count.\n- Why: the tripwire **doesn't cap spend**; the budget still does that. It **bounds retries of one kind of failure**, and it makes you notice a broken delivery path while you still have budget to fix it. Unlimited retries hide a systematic failure behind the budget.\n- *Story from VizH's v0.9.6 job:* 9 of 10 Maker attempts failed at \"the diff does not apply\" (hunk counts off by 1 to 3). A limit on attempts is what made them see it was the transport, not the model. Unlimited retries would have spent the budget on it.\n- *Ours:* gen 2 returned four corrupt diffs and none applied. We changed the contract to whole files (section 3, item 3) after one round, but nothing in the rules forced that. The `finish=length` rule (section 4) is the same idea, applied on the first occurrence because a retry can't change that outcome.\n\n**b) Keep the shared OpenRouter key, with the spend guard. Reconciliation is then an upper bound.**\n- Steve: \"Keep the shared key with the spend guard.\" (This confirms his 2026-10-28 11:58 EDT choice, t82u: \"Use the same openrouter key I gave you last time.\")\n- What to do: every paid call goes through the guarded `or_call.py`. Before freeze, initialize its baseline and test the refusal path. In the record, report spend as the guard's figure plus a key-usage reconciliation, labeled as an **upper bound**, with the shared-key gaps below disclosed.\n- What the guard and reserve **cover**: it refuses a call (exit 6, nothing sent) when spent + the call's worst case + a $0.05 reserve ≥ the bound. \"Spent\" is the larger of the job's own ledger and the key's usage change since the job's baseline. The worst case is prompt bytes × input price + max tokens × output price, so no single call can cross the bound. Reserves for unsettled calls count as spent. Only priced (model, provider) pairs can be called. It refuses when key usage can't be read.\n- What it **doesn't** cover, so say so in the record: (1) spend that bypasses `or_call.py` (another tool, bot or job on the key) is counted after the fact, not stopped; (2) other spend on the key counts against the job, so the job's key-usage figure is an **upper bound, not exact**, and a busy key can refuse a legitimate call; (3) key usage lags, so read it at least 2–3 minutes after the last call; (4) there's no provider-side ceiling as a backstop if the guard has a bug.\n- Why: VizH strongly recommended a per-job key with a provider-enforced limit, because it makes the ceiling real and reconciliation exact. Steve weighed that and chose the shared key. The guard gives the job a hard bound in the tool, and the disclosure keeps the record honest about what that bound is.\n- *Story from VizH's v0.9.6/v0.9.7 jobs:* their shared key's total and their job ledger never reconciled, and the key's own limit stopped a call partway through.\n- *Ours:* the doctrine-pointer job ran this way. Spend was $0.014041 on the ledger and the same as the key-usage change (0.303493375 → 0.317534503), so nothing else hit the key during the job. With the key shared, that match was luck, not a guarantee.\n\n**c) A pre-read by a different model family is optional, decided per job.**\n- Steve: \"Make the cross-family pre-read optional, decided per job.\"\n- What to do: put it on every approval sheet as a numbered item with your recommendation (yes for a new kind of bar or new instruments, usually no for a small repeat of a known shape). If Steve says yes, a reviewer from a model family not already in the job reads the bar, the runbook and the instruments before freeze and lists what's unclear or unfair. Fix or justify every finding in a file committed with the job, and repeat until a round adds nothing new.\n- Why: a reader from outside catches wording and gate problems the author can't see. It costs a round of spend and time, so Steve decides it per job. It **can't** catch a starved model, so it never replaces the live run (section 3, item 8).\n- *Story from VizH's v0.9.6/v0.9.7 jobs:* successive pre-read rounds returned 35, then 38, then 4, then 3, then 0 findings, and the Architect fixed or justified each one.\n\n## Job 2026-10-28-doctrine-reader-pointer: running notes\n- **Runbooks must say \"write the files\", not just \"check them\".** MAKER.md iter 1 went from `extract_files.py --check` (writes nothing) straight to \"commit\". The Maker spotted it and ran the same command without `--check` to write the reply verbatim, then committed. That was right, but the runbook should never leave the Maker to guess. Rule: every handoff step that produces bytes names the command that writes them.\n- **Handoffs written by a helper can quietly drift from standing rules.** The iter-1 MAKER.md capped Maker retries at one and committed as Steve. Both contradict standing rules: the cap counts E-DI iterations, and the Maker is author with Steve as committer. I caught both and overrode them in the relay. Rule: before sending a handoff, check it against the profile rules (no throttle, retry policy, git identity, no /tmp).\n- **A shared key turns key-usage spend into an upper bound, not an exact figure.** Steve chose to reuse the existing key instead of a $2-limited per-job key. The guard measures the key's usage change, so anything else on the key counts against the job. We kept a $0.05 reserve.  Steve kept this as the rule on 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT; see section 10b.\n```\n\n## DOCTRINE.md v0.1 @ dc61854 (evaluatordriven/doctrine, CC BY 4.0; the guide must not contradict it)\n\n```\n# Evaluator Driven\n\n**E-DI: Evaluator-Driven Iteration. A doctrine for delegating work so that quality survives.**\n\nVersion 0.1 · ratified 2026-10-25 · Steve Moitozo II · Licence: CC BY 4.0\n\n---\n\n## 0. In one paragraph\n\n**Can I trust our name to this work?** A decision-maker asks that before the company's name goes on work an AI agent produced. A practitioner asks the same of their own name. Evaluator Driven exists so the honest and confident answer\ncan be yes.\n\nWork made by an agent, a model or a person is trusted only as far as it has been judged. Evaluator-Driven work is assessed against a standard that was set before the work began: the bar.\nIt's judged by a qualified Evaluator whose ability to properly judge work has been tested AND who did not\nproduce the work itself. And the record of the work can be checked by anyone who needs to audit it. The evaluation drives the\nwork. It’s not simply paperwork after the fact.\nWhen the work falls short, the Evaluator reports the deficiencies, with proof. The Maker then revises\nagainst exactly that. The Orchestrator ensures the loop stops in a named way, within bounds set in advance, and that the person accountable for the outcome\nkeeps the decisions only they can make. And their decision gets written beside the verdict, never over it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why this exists\n\n**Who it is for.** Three readers:\n\n- the **decision-maker**, who sanctions handing a kind of work to AI and must put the company's name on the result;\n- the **practitioner**, who already uses AI and must put their own name on what it helped make;\n- **the practitioner's AI agent**, which needs a published standard to work to, not its own improvisation.\n\nTwo parties are not readers but **authorities**: the **Buyer**, whose fitness test the work must pass,\nand the **Subject-matter Expert**, whose quality test it must pass.\nTheir standard of “well done” is the one that counts. ⟨Owner, 2026-10-08⟩\n\n**The pain is one defect with three faces: quality that cannot be accounted for.** ⟨Owner, 2026-10-08⟩\n\n- **The signature.** You cannot put a name on work whose quality you cannot account for.\n- **Guilt.** The practitioner got a good result and cannot say what they did to get it. It feels as if the\n  AI produced quality by accident, so they keep quiet about how the work was made.\n- **Delegation that does not survive.** A method that lives in one person's head dies when it is handed\n  to a colleague, a team or an agent. Nobody needs a standard to do their own work; you need one the\n  moment somebody else has to do it too.\n\n**The decision-maker's question is a comparison.** Not \"is AI good?\" but \"will the result be at least as\nacceptable as what it replaces?\" And the decision-maker wants to see that for themselves, not take it on faith.\nEvaluator Driven answers it the only honest way. Hold the work to the bar you would hold your people's\nwork to: the same Subject-matter Expert's standard, the same Buyer's test. Set that bar before the work begins and have the work judged by an Evaluator who did not make it. Work that passes has been judged to meet that bar, and the record lets\nyou check it yourself. Work that does not goes back to the Maker or comes to you, the Owner. ⟨Owner, 2026-10-08; evaluatordriven.com⟩\n\n**The acceptance principle.** In the Owner's words:\n\n> If the work passes the quality test of a subject matter expert and the fitness test of an end-user,\n> shouldn't it be acceptable regardless of the tools used to produce it?\n\nThat is the stricter claim, not the softer one. A standard that judged only methods could be satisfied by\nusing approved tools badly. A standard that judges the work against named tests leaves nothing to\nhide behind, and makes admitting the tool a detail rather than a confession. ⟨Owner, 2026-10-08⟩\n\n**The big bet.** The same job, run inside a well-managed and well-judged loop, beats the same job run without one. The corollary, if the bet holds, is what makes it matter: quality comes from the judging and detailed feedback more than from the size of the Maker.\nSo, a modest, cheap, even locally hosted model inside the loop can produce work that passes the bar. This\nis why Evaluator Driven exists. §8 sets out the evidence so far, and what would settle it.\n⟨Mandate §8, B.1.5; Owner, 2026-10-24⟩\n\n**Why now.** Generation used to be the expensive step, so outputs were checked carefully. Now producing work is close to free, and the binding constraint is the judging. The work is still checked. What changes is that the checking itself can now be delegated with confidence, once it meets a standard of its own: a bar set first, an Evaluator tested before it serves, and a record anyone can audit. The person who gets quality from agents is the one who\ninvests more in the Evaluator and loop discipline than in the Maker. Delegation fails in known ways. When \"done\" and \"good\" are unclear, even human delegation suffers. The work cannot be checked fairly. The worker lacks the skill. The feedback is late or useless.\nOr the work is graded by its Maker, or by judges who know neither the Buyer's need nor what masterful\nwork looks like.\n⟨Primer, \"Why this exists\"; Mandate P2; Owner, 2026-10-25⟩\n\n**The practice is old; the name is new.** Good editors, managers and craftsmen have always defined done\nbefore handing off. They checked the work against the standard, not the worker's account of it. They sent\nit back with specific feedback, and kept the final call with the person whose name is on it. Agents work\nwithout you in the room, so your standards survive your absence only if they are written down. ⟨Primer⟩\n\n**Neighbouring methods cover part of the ground.** A specification tells an agent what to build; it does\nnot show that the agent built it. A proof covers what a formal specification can state; most work (prose,\ntranslation, design, data, judgement calls) cannot be proved. Evaluator Driven is for the rest.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The grounding axiom: the Evaluator is a proxy, never a principal\n\nBefore any mechanics, one question governs everything:\n\n> **Who is buying this output, and which master of the craft approved the bar?**\n\nQuality is not a platonic property a rubric can point at. It lives with two authorities, and the bar sits\nwhere they meet:\n\n- the **Buyer** defines fitness for purpose: does this solve my problem, within my constraints?\n- the **Specialist**, the subject-matter expert, defines excellence: would a master of the craft accept\n  this?\n\nThey can pull against each other, and judgement must hold both. **The bar is the intersection of the Buyer's need and\nthe Specialist's standard.** ⟨Primer, grounding axiom; Playbook, seats⟩\n\nSo an Evaluator holds authority on loan. Its standard must carry provenance to both, written down as four\nfacts:\n\n1. who the Buyer is;\n2. what they need, and which constraints they named;\n3. whose standard of excellence is borrowed;\n4. where that standard lives, versioned.\n\nA rubric that cannot answer those four is a preference wearing a score. **No provenance, no authority.** ⟨Primer; Instrument rule 1⟩\n\nTwo consequences follow. **You can delegate the doing; you can never delegate responsibility for the\nstandard.** And **the doctrine improves how well work meets its frame; it does not set the frame.** Values,\npriorities, risk appetite and taste belong to the Owner. ⟨Primer; Mandate X1–X3⟩\n\n---\n\n## 3. The seats\n\nA seat is a responsibility, not a person. One person or model may hold several seats if that is\ndisclosed, with one exception that is never waived: **the Maker never judges its own work.**\n\n| Seat | Does | Never does |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Owner** | Owns the outcome. Ratifies the frame. Sets values, priority and risk appetite. Decides what the loop cannot. May override a verdict, recorded as a disposition beside it. | Rewrites a verdict. |\n| **Architect** | Defines done and good, writes the standard, sets the thresholds and bounds, and builds the instruments. | Writes or tunes the standard after seeing a draft. |\n| **Buyer** | Is the one whose problem is being solved. States the need and the constraints. | — |\n| **Specialist** | Supplies the standard of excellence for the craft. | — |\n| **Maker** | Produces the work and revises it against classified feedback. | Judges its own output, or produces the observation it is judged by. |\n| **Evaluator** | Judges the work against the frozen standard, through a channel the Maker did not produce, and returns a structured verdict. | Produces or repairs the work. |\n| **Orchestrator** | Runs the loop: dispatch, feedback, bounds, the record. | Writes the work or scores it. |\n\n⟨Playbook §6; Primer, \"The seats, named\"; Mandate X4⟩\n\n**Two forms of the Evaluator seat** have earned names in practice:\n\n- **The adjudicator** is an Evaluator who rules on a disagreement between Evaluators. It reads the work\n  itself (the code, the text). An adjudicator never resolves a split by averaging.\n- **The test author** writes an independent channel: a test, a planted case, a task for a reader who has\n  never seen the work. The test author is not the Maker and does not see the Maker's reasoning.\n\n⟨practice⟩\n\n**On families of models.** Seats may share an engine, a vendor or a model family. What may not happen is\nan Evaluator seat biased toward the Maker's family. Two models from one family tend to share blind spots, and an Evaluator that shares the Maker's blind spots is the Maker grading itself by another route. So:\n\n- at least one Evaluator differs from the Maker in model family;\n- on every dimension, the Maker's family holds no more than half of the Evaluators;\n- every seat's holder is disclosed, and an undisclosed model family is read the least favourable way: as the Maker's.\n\n⟨custody record v0.9.6, CL2-3; practice⟩\n\n---\n\n## 4. Principles\n\n1. **The bar comes first.** Done and good exist before the first draft. A definition written after seeing a draft is an anchor on the draft, not a standard. For existing work (Entry B), the bar comes before the judging instead, and declaration order stands in for blindness. ⟨Mandate M1, M1.1; Primer, core sequence⟩\n\n2. **Build the Evaluator before the Maker produces, and show it catching a bad case.** Write the instrument, run it on a case it must fail (the work's own real failure if there is one, otherwise a deliberately bad case), then freeze and hash it. If you have never seen the instrument say no, its yes means nothing. ⟨Instrument A2, rule 3, Entry B's thirteenth rule⟩\n\n3. **The Maker never grades its own work, and never produces the observation it's graded on.** The\n   Evaluator sees the work. The observation comes through a channel the Maker did not produce and cannot\n   easily fake. Examples: tests the author did not write; a back-translation by a reader who never saw the\n   source; a stranger following the instructions cold. ⟨Mandate M2; Primer, move 2; Instrument rule 2⟩\n\n4. **Anchor to ground truth, and name how hard it is.** Judge against the source and known-good\n   references, never the Maker's working copy. Ground truth comes in kinds: an invariant, a document of\n   record, a prior. No dimension may claim more certainty than the channel that carries it. ⟨Primer, move 2;\n   Instrument rule 8⟩\n\n5. **Judges are tested too.** Every Evaluator, in every batch, reads at least one case that should pass and\n   one that should fail. The model serving a seat is part of the instrument, and its strictness,\n   stability and cost are measured, not assumed. A verdict rests on at least two scored runs; one run is a\n   sample. A judge that is never tested can name a defect and then excuse it, or report checking text that is not there; planted cases are how you find out. Put cheap deterministic checks (length,\n   structure, required elements) in front of the judge: they catch what a lenient judge waves through.\n   And measure each Evaluator's spread on identical input: a reading that changes between identical runs is\n   the instrument's noise, so only what holds across runs counts as a finding.\n   ⟨Instrument rule 10, Entry B's thirteenth rule; Contracts §10.6; practice⟩\n\n6. **Disagreement is information; never average it away.** A scored pass needs both an average threshold and a floor on every dimension, because an average alone lets a fatal defect hide. Readings from\n   different Evaluators are never averaged: where they agree within the channel's measured spread, the\n   agreement is the reading; where they split, the split is shown and adjudicated. A disagreement between\n   the work and the standard is traced to its cause, never written off as a bad Maker. Either the work missed the\n   standard, the standard moved, the dimension was never authorized, or the frame itself is at fault.\n   ⟨Contracts §3, §10.6; Instrument rule 4; practice⟩\n\n7. **Feedback carries the shortfall, always; a remedy only with real insight.** Say how the work falls\n   short and quote the evidence. Offer a remedy where the Evaluator genuinely sees one. An invented remedy is\n   worse than none; a measured remedy withheld is also a defect. A remedy points the way; it is never the fix. The Evaluator does not write the revision, and the Maker owns how the shortfall is fixed. An Evaluator that writes the fix grades its own work on the next round. A score with no observation behind it is not\n   a score. And \"could not observe\" is a different fact from \"found missing\": what an Evaluator cannot see\n   is recorded as unobserved, never as absent. ⟨Mandate M3; Instrument rule 3; Contracts §5; practice⟩\n\n8. **Feedback must be able to change the next version, or do not loop.** If the Maker cannot act on the\n   judgement, iteration is theatre. Identical outputs mean the influence channel is dormant, not weak. A\n   capability gap escalates; it is not retried. ⟨Mandate M3.6, M4; Primer, move 3; Instrument rule 5⟩\n\n9. **Inside a loop, a change answers only what the Evaluator reported.** Everything else waits for the next\n   bar. Carry the best work forward: a revision replaces the incumbent only if it is better on at least one\n   dimension and worse on none. A trade between dimensions is the Owner's call, never the loop's.\n   ⟨Primer, worked example; Instrument rule 12⟩\n\n10. **Bound the loop, and name every exit.** A cap, a budget and a time limit are supplied, never defaulted,\n    and bind while the run proceeds. Every path ends in a named state. ⟨Mandate M5; Instrument rule 6⟩\n\n11. **Escalate without interrogation.** What can be found out, the agents find out. Values, priority, risk\n    and the Buyer's intent go to the Owner, with what was tried, why no observable answer exists, and the\n    options with a recommendation. A bare question is work pushed back, not an escalation. **The Owner's\n    attention is a budget, and it is measured.** There are three signals, each with a target of zero. The\n    same kind of approval asked again and again. Choices put without a recommendation. Answers the Owner has\n    to ask to have re-explained. A system that leaves its Owner feeling worked by the machine has failed, however good\n    its output. ⟨Mandate M6, F5; Instrument, \"Report where the frame failed\"; practice⟩\n\n12. **Evidence or it did not happen.** Every claim cites a file, kept by its content hash. Notes are written\n    from what the instruments produced, never from what someone intended. Keep the instrument that\n    accepted the work, and the record, with the work: committed beside it, not in local scratch. Pin every\n    standard by hash or commit, never by a version name alone; a version number cannot be checked. Figures,\n    hashes and record fields are printed by the tool that produced them, never retyped by hand.\n    ⟨Mandate M2, M5, F6; Instrument rule 10; practice⟩\n\n13. **Proportion.** The instrument is as heavy as the risk and no heavier. A smaller instrument is a recorded\n    choice. It is never partial: it may not skip a must-ask value, a terminal state or a checked line.\n    ⟨Mandate M7; Instrument rule 11⟩\n\n14. **The level of delegation changes who decides, never what must be supplied.** Declare the rung (do\n    exactly this; research and report; research and recommend; decide and inform; act). No rung waives a\n    must-ask. ⟨Mandate M8; Instrument rule 9⟩\n\n---\n\n## 5. The loop\n\n**Four moves, each one an answer for the person who signs:** ⟨Owner, 2026-10-08⟩\n\n| Move | What it lets the signer say |\n|---|---|\n| **Set the bar** | I decided what \"good\" and \"done\" would mean before I saw the result. |\n| **Produce against it** | The method is written down, so it can happen again, in someone else's hands. |\n| **Evaluate independently** | Somebody who did not make the output checked it. |\n| **Keep the record** | My name is attached, and so is everything it rests on. |\n\nBehind them sits the core sequence: define quality and done, build the Evaluator, build the Maker, then run the loop. Every loop that works runs in that order. You cannot evaluate what you have not defined. ⟨Primer, core sequence⟩ And a\ndefinition written after seeing a draft is anchored on the draft.\n\n### Two entries\n\n**Commissioned work (Entry A)** does not exist yet. The standard is written first, so it cannot have been\ntuned to the artifact. Before generating, the Maker states the win and the done rule back in its own\nwords: the cheapest defect filter there is. ⟨Instrument, Entry A⟩\n\n**Existing work (Entry B, retrofit)** is already made, perhaps already in use. You cannot be blind to an\nanswer that exists, so declaration order substitutes for blindness. First collect the work's own standard:\nits docs, tests and claims. Then declare the frame you would hold it to now, and freeze the instrument.\nOnly then read anything that could reveal a defect. The record says which reads came before the freeze.\nThe past gets a census, not a verdict, and thresholds apply forward. The richest finding is usually the\nwork contradicting itself: claims made but not implemented, and behaviour implemented but not claimed.\n⟨Instrument, Entry B⟩\n\n### One turn of the loop\n\n1. **Freeze.** The bar, the instruments and the bounds are fixed and hashed. The work starts after.\n2. **Make.** The Maker produces a version.\n3. **Observe.** Independent channels produce observations the Maker did not touch.\n4. **Judge.** Evaluator(s) score or read each dimension, each score with its quoted observation, alongside\n   their controls. The verdict is a structure, not prose: dimensions, violations (each classified, each\n   quoting its evidence), and a recommendation of accept, regenerate, needs owner, or reject.\n5. **Decide the step.** Pass the rule: stop. Fall short: send the classified shortfall back to the Maker\n   as constraints. Nothing else goes back.\n6. **Revise and repeat**, within the bounds.\n\n⟨Primer, \"The evaluator's contract\", \"The run packet\"; Example⟩\n\n### The exits\n\nA job ends in one of three states: **accepted**, **needs owner**, or **rejected**.\n\n- **Accepted:** the work passed the rule fixed before it began.\n- **Needs owner:** the cap was reached without a pass while the work stayed above the reject floor, or an escalation fired, or the instrument failed. Below the cap, such work goes back to the Maker. The **best version** the job produced goes to the Owner, never to the bin.\n- **Rejected:** below the reject floor. Work not yet in use is discarded. Work already in use is never\n  discarded by the loop; the verdict stands and what to do is the Owner's call.\n\nBeside the state, the record carries the outcome that explains it. For example:\n\n- *census*: readings only, because no Specialist was named when the job began, so there is no bar to score against; every readable dimension must conform, or the job goes to the Owner;\n- *no observation*: the instrument failed. That is a finding about the instrument, never a zero for the\n  work;\n- *no artifact*: the Maker produced nothing.\n\nThere is no state in which a loop quietly ends. ⟨Mandate M5.2; Instrument, Exits⟩\n\n**Verdict and disposition are separate acts.** The Owner may ship a rejected piece or shelve an accepted\none. The verdict stays as the loop produced it; the decision is written beside it. ⟨Mandate X4;\nInstrument rule 7⟩\n\n### Where the human sits\n\nOn well-trodden ground, where an Evaluator has a validation record and calibrated thresholds, the loop\nruns without a person and exceptions surface as *needs owner*. On novel ground (a new domain, new stakes, a\nfirst run), a person stays in every turn until the proxy earns trust. The mistake is not keeping a human too\nlong; it is removing one before the proxy has proved itself. This is human-at-the-helm, not just human-in-the-loop: the person sets the bar before the work begins, keeps the decisions only they can make, and decides when the loop has earned the right to run without them. ⟨Primer, \"The human stays terminal\"⟩\n\n---\n\n## 6. Qualifying the seats\n\nSeat no one on reputation, including the Architect.\n\n- **Test every seat on planted cases before it serves.** A Maker shows it can generate and, separately,\n  revise; an Evaluator shows it can fail what it judges; an Orchestrator shows it stops when the runbook\n  says stop. The Architect's seat is not yet tested this way; that gap is open. ⟨Instrument rule 10; practice⟩\n- **Check every instrument against a valid alternative.** Before an instrument scores anything, run it on a\n  correct solution built differently from the one its author had in mind. An instrument that fails a valid\n  alternative is measuring the author's design, not the bar. ⟨practice⟩\n- **Have someone independent pre-read the instruments.** The Architect writes the runbook and the scorer;\n  an adjudicator reads both against the bar before any work starts. Where the instrument and the bar\n  differ, the bar governs. ⟨practice⟩\n- **Pin instruments before use**, and keep them by hash. A rerun with the same rubric and a different\n  runner is a different instrument. ⟨Instrument rule 10⟩\n\nBuilding the custody record showed why. In v0.9.5, two of the Architect's scoring tools scored a preferred design rather than the rules. In v0.9.6 it happened again: the Orchestrator found a tool that would have failed a valid design the brief allowed, and the work was scored again with a corrected tool, both scorings kept. The rules on valid alternatives and independent pre-reads exist to catch that before anything is scored. ⟨custody record v0.9.6: BAR-v0.9.6, theme; qualification-v0.9.6/QUAL-AMENDMENT-1⟩\n\n---\n\n## 7. The record\n\nThe record is what lets a person sign with integrity and confidence: it shows what the work was measured against, who checked it\nindependently, and who accepted it. A loop that leaves no record cannot be trusted by anyone who was not in the room when it was made. The record is how the doctrine reaches someone who wasn’t there to witness the work. It shows what the bar was and who held which seat. It\nshows what each Evaluator saw, how they disagreed, what the Maker changed, and how the job ended. Accepted work that cannot be reconstructed from\nits records counts as a failure of the doctrine (§8, F6). ⟨Mandate M5, F6⟩\n\nThe interoperable form of that record is the **Evaluator Driven custody record**, `custody/v1`: a\nhash-chained log and an in-toto predicate, with conformance levels a checker can verify. It is a separate,\nneutral specification with its own repository. It is still a candidate: its v0.9.6 stranger test did not pass, and no product loop yet emits `custody/v1`. ⟨custody record v0.9.6⟩ The record states facts; this doctrine says\nwhich facts matter and why. **The doctrine never redefines the record's terms**, and the record never\ndepends on the doctrine. A job run under another method can still produce a valid record. The record will\nshow how far that job met the practices above.\n\n---\n\n## 8. How you would know it failed\n\nThe doctrine names its own failure signals, so it can be judged by them. ⟨Mandate F1–F6⟩\n\n- **F1.** Work ships with no Buyer in the record who asked for it.\n- **F2.** Judging costs more than the errors it catches (on a fair comparison).\n- **F3.** Work scored above the bar is later rejected by the named Buyer or Specialist, and the record\n  cannot say which dimension failed.\n- **F4.** Two honest runs of the same setup disagree by more than the instrument's normal variation.\n- **F5.** The Owner is asked questions the system could have answered itself. Or the Owner is forced to carry an attention cost the system could have spared: repeated approvals of the same kind, choices without a\n  recommendation, answers that need re-explaining. ⟨Mandate F5; practice⟩\n- **F6.** Accepted work cannot be reconstructed from its records.\n\n**What is claimed is repeatability, not scale.** The bar, the instruments and the record exist so a result\ncan be reproduced by someone else. Nothing yet supports a claim that the method scales, so none is made. Repeatability is designed in, not yet shown: no result here is known to have been reproduced by someone else.\n⟨Owner, 2026-10-08⟩\n\n**The evidence for the big bet, so far.** ⟨Mandate §8, B.1.5; evidence/software-defect-pairs.md; evaluation records⟩\n\n| Case | Maker | What was compared | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A translation system for low-resource languages | Code: a 9B model, hosted locally, writing to tests written first by a cloud-hosted model. Translation: a 1.3B model, hosted locally | The build: the loop only. The judging: an audit against planted defects, run independently of the judges under test | 900 of 911 tests pass offline with a dummy key set; 10 of the 11 failures are a real broken integrity gate. Spanish content made in one pass, outside the loop, carried real grammar errors in 3 of 100 units, which its review missed. The production judge, tested on planted defects, accepted 9 of 9 in its production mode; a simple structure check caught 6 of the 9, and one escaped every automated check. On 2026-09-06, two Lingala speakers read the Lingala output and accepted it: a first-language speaker reviewed 10 days of devotional content, and a second-language speaker read 5 (a retrospective report: which days, and how they read, are not recorded). One project, not a paired comparison |\n| Seven real software defects in two web applications | One locally hosted 35B-class model, the same on both arms | One pass on all seven; the loop only on the 3 the pass missed. An executable probe as the pass rule (in one row a failing held test also counted as a miss), and a second Evaluator from another family agreeing on all seven | One pass fixed 4. Of the 3 it missed, the loop fixed 2 and missed 1. The Owner named this *a* comparator for software defect resolution, not *the* comparator |\n| One live defect in a sign-in code endpoint | A 4B model, hosted locally | One pass versus the loop, with a second Evaluator agreeing | One pass missed; the loop was accepted on its fourth generation. Earlier loops on this defect, at temperature 0, did not reach acceptance and are not counted. Loop judging cost under half a cent, or $0.0139 with the second Evaluator's check. A hosted model fixed the same defect in one pass for $0.0140, with no second check: which is cheaper depends on what is counted. The Owner reports the defect cost about $20 in production; whether judging pays for itself (F2) is still open |\n| A mobile devotional app (Android) | A 9B model, hosted locally, writing code to failing tests written first by the Evaluator; a cloud-hosted model from another family reviewed the diffs | The loop only; no run without it | A signed Android release build, with the unit, contract and browser suites recorded green (2026-08-29). The outside review caught 4 serious defects the tests had missed, and each was fixed through the loop. Files were later changed outside the loop, with no gate, and the archived source now fails its own tests: the loop protects only what passes through it. No device test was run, iOS was never built, and the release APK probably lacks its privacy policy. One project, not a paired comparison |\n\n**Three conditions on the bet. The first two were learned the hard way in the translation work, the third in the mobile app.**\n\n1. **The feedback must be able to change the work.** The first translation loop (2026-09-03) gave its\n   translation model feedback that model could not act on. Of 25 jobs, 1 was accepted. Seven Lingala jobs never produced a score; the rest wandered, mostly between 1 and 4, without converging. Nine of 12 Swahili jobs scored exactly 7.8 on both iterations, with the same one-line feedback: consistent with a dormant influence channel. The next day a step was added that rewrote the source in\n   response to the Evaluator's complaints. A different 20-row batch then passed 17 of 20 at a threshold of 7.5, under the same loop's own judge. The main change was a way to act on the judgement. That judge was later found to pass planted defects (condition 2), so the rise shows the loop could act on its judge, not how far quality rose.\n   ⟨Primer, move 3; practice⟩\n2. **The judge must be qualified.** The later audit shows the second: a loop with an untested Evaluator\n   passes defects with confidence. Well-judged is something you measure with planted cases (§6), not something you assume.\n3. **The work must stay inside the loop.** Changes made outside it are neither judged nor recorded. In the\n   mobile app, files were later changed with no gate, and the archived source now fails its own tests. ⟨mobile-app review, 2026-10-24⟩\n\nOnly the software-defect pair and the Lingala reading are published (evidence/). The other translation and mobile-app records are the Owner's and not yet published; read those rows as his report.\n\nThe bet is on a loop that meets all three.\n\n**What would settle it:** paired jobs in more than one domain. Each pair keeps the same Buyer, the same\nstandard and the same class of work, and runs once with the loop and once without. Each is published with\nits record, and the results are reported as a rate that counts the misses as well as the wins, with outcomes beyond the pass: use, early detection and cost. Until then the bet is supported in software, shown working but not compared in translation, and not yet established across domains. This document says so rather than borrowing\nauthority it has not earned. ⟨Mandate B.1.5⟩\n\n---\n\n## 9. What this doctrine is not\n\n- **Not a method for making things.** Make whatever products you like with whatever tools you like: by specification, by proof, by hand, by agent. Evaluator Driven governs how the work is judged, fed back and carried, and so it shapes the loop the Maker works in.\n- **Not a source of values.** It does not set values, priorities, risk appetite or taste; the Owner does,\n  and the doctrine then scores against that standard. ⟨Mandate X1–X3⟩\n- **Not a replacement for the Owner's override**, which is recorded beside the verdict, never over it.\n  ⟨Mandate X4⟩\n- **Not a product.** It is a set of rules you adopt, not a product you buy. ⟨evaluatordriven.com⟩\n- **Not certification.** A record shows how a judgement was made, not that the work is correct. ⟨custody record, SPEC §1⟩\n- **Not finished.** It is versioned, and it changes when a job shows that it must, not before. No rule\n  without a job that needed it. ⟨Instrument, Refusals⟩\n\n---\n\n## 10. Lineage\n\n**Origin.** The doctrine was first written down on 2026-08-31 as \"Evaluator Driven Iteration\": define\nquality first, build the evaluator before the generator, close the loop. ⟨Primer, references⟩ It grew into a corpus of seven weight-bearing documents: a quickstart, a playbook, an operating instrument, machine contracts, a mandate, a worked example and a guide for machines. A primer teaches it but is not weight-bearing. The Owner ratified each document and its later amendments, and the set is held at commit `a48a464`. The Primer was itself revised under the method: Evaluators from five model families, none of them the reviser, scoring against an instrument written before the revision existed. The corpus is private; these facts are the Owner's record of it. ⟨Playbook, load map; Primer, revision note v1.1⟩\n\n**This articulation.** Version 0.1 restates the corpus for readers who will never open it. It adds the\nlessons of the custody-record releases v0.9.4 to v0.9.6, marked ⟨practice⟩ above. Its rules: test Evaluators with planted cases in every batch; never average disagreements; check instruments against valid alternatives and pre-read them before use; keep evidence by hash, and write notes from output, not intent. Those\nreleases were themselves run under this doctrine, and their records are public: https://github.com/smoitozo/evaluatordriven, tags v0.9.4 to v0.9.6. One rule departs from the\ncorpus on purpose. The corpus barred the Maker's family from the Evaluator seat outright. This version adopts the custody record's principle: the seat must not be biased toward the Maker's family (§3). The record's rule, CL2-3, also requires a different endpoint.\n\n**What it stands on.** Covey's stewardship delegation (desired results, guidelines, resources,\naccountability, consequences). Hyatt's five levels of delegation. The writer–reviewer split in agent\nworkflows. The finding that models correcting themselves with only internal reasoning improve little or\nget worse, while structured external feedback helps (Gou et al., *CRITIC*, 2023). The constitutional\nform: a written, versioned standard, critiques that cite the clause they enforce, and revisions that\nanswer the citation. Evaluation-driven development: a scorecard before optimization, dimensions rather\nthan a pooled average. Teach-back from clinical practice: the person repeats the instruction in their own\nwords, because \"do you understand?\" reliably gets a yes. ⟨Instrument, \"What we stand on\"; Primer, move 1⟩\n\n**Applied to itself.** This document had to pass the gate it describes. The Architect drafted it for the Owner's edit; a reader from another model family checked it for claims its evidence does not support; the Owner ratified it on 2026-10-25.\n\n---\n\n*Evaluator Driven, v0.1. © 2026 Steve Moitozo II. Licensed under CC BY 4.0.\nevaluatordriven.com*\n```\n\nReturn your files now, following the OUTPUT CONTRACT exactly.\n"
