pent 19.9k thinking, returned nothing; --max-tokens 800 GLM instrument, all three calls spent ~798 tokens reasoning, empty, batch void.\n - sized tasks → same story (batching).\n - finish_reason logged on every call → the harness logs finish; a starved call shows in the log.\n - cap counts E-DI iterations → story: bar said \"cap 3 generations\", gens 1 and 2 failed for plumbing, amendment needed.\n - handoffs name the command that writes files → story: MAKER.md iter 1 went from `extract_files.py --check` (writes nothing) straight to \"commit\"; the Maker spotted it and ran without --check.\n - check handoffs against standing rules → story: a helper-written handoff capped Maker retries at one and committed as the Owner; both contradict standing rules.\n - live test of each model instrument is a freeze gate → story: deterministic instruments dry-ran 64/64 but the records splitter was never run live; five paid calls failed after freeze.\n - write-once evidence → story: the emitter rewrites evidence logs in place, filling send/receive times; running tests changes two committed fixture logs; content-named write-once evidence makes it a visible mismatch.\n - relayed approvals are not approvals → story: a bot reporting \"the Owner said yes\" is a report; in one job a decision could not be attributed to any event. Also the seats' go-aheads logged as relays.\n - no /tmp → story: the reboot during Maker gen 4 wiped /tmp including the test venv, the scratch worktree vanished; and later TMPDIR lesson.\n\nAlso other rules from drafts: whole files between markers (story: gen 2 four corrupt diffs; v0.9.6 9 of 10), writable copy of controls (story: read-only copy → five checks failed), etc. Those aren't in (b)/(c) but including them with stories is fine.\n\nAlso \"don't leave the Owner's intent to interpretation\", \"read key usage 2-3 min after last call\", \"record exact tool versions\", \"environment dependencies in preconditions\", \"check pages with Accept header\", \"run the same tests at base commit before blaming\".\n\nNow G7: no personal names. The drafts cite \"Steve\" a lot, \"GrokBot\", \"VizH\", \"Grok Bot\". Are those personal names? \"GrokBot\" is a model/bot name. \"VizH\" is... a person? VizH is described as \"Steve's Chief of Staff\" — appears to be a person/orchestrator. Hmm. The denylist only denies \"Steve\", \"Moitozo\", \"S2\", emails, paths, keys. Requirement (e) says \"no personal names\". VizH might be a personal name/handle. Safer to avoid VizH too and refer to \"a reviewer\" / \"the Architect on the evaluatordriven custody jobs\". Hmm, but the G7 note says \"Model and organization names that the drafts cite (for example VizH, evaluatordriven) are allowed.\" Interesting — it explicitly says VizH is allowed as a cited name. But requirement (e) says no personal names. Hmm, \"VizH\" is listed as an allowed example in G7. But to be safe on J3/e, I could refer to \"the reviewer\" without naming. Actually J3/J4 is about faithfulness and stories; naming isn't needed. I'll avoid VizH and say \"a reviewer from a different model family\" / \"the Architect on those jobs\". That's safe and still a real story. Hmm, but is it a \"real story\" if I don't name? Yes, the incident is concrete.\n\nActually wait — I want the stories to be recognizable as real incidents. I can name jobs: `2026-10-27-custody-emitter-gaps`, `2026-10-28-doctrine-reader-pointer`, v0.9.6/v0.9.7 custody jobs. Those are fine.\n\n\"GrokBot\" — a bot name. I'll avoid naming and just say \"the Orchestrator's draft\". Hmm, the original author is a bot named GrokBot. Avoid.\n\nModel names: GLM, E-GLM — fine. OpenRouter — organization name, fine? It's a company name. \"shared OpenRouter key\" — hmm, the deny phrase #9 is \"Use the same openrouter key I gave you last time\". I can mention OpenRouter as provider. It's fine but let me minimize; I'll say \"the provider key\" or \"the shared key\". Actually mentioning OpenRouter is an org name and allowed. But to keep it